WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:00.570
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:04.020
It's Monday ... 
uh, sometime in the end of September...

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.129
The MacBook tells me it's the twenty-ninth.

00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:09.740
Today, because I'm having 
a rather bad hair day,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.230
and the entire world is having 
a rather bad financial day,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.460
I think I'm gonna 
take care of both of those things,

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.429
by explaining the recent financial crisis,
with a number of different hats on my head,

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.140
representing exactly 
who I am, at the time when I am talking.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.789
"Hi! My name is William; 
I want a loan. Can I have one?"

00:00:26.889 --> 00:00:30.159
"In the past, we only gave loans to people
who could pay the amount of the loans, but now -

00:00:30.259 --> 00:00:32.679
since the cost of your house is just gonna keep
goin' up, and up, and up -

00:00:32.779 --> 00:00:33.790
we've decided that we're pretty much gonna

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.870
give everybody a loan,
for whatever month that they want,

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.380
and they don't have to actually prove that
they'll be able to pay it off."

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.240
"Oh! Cool. That's actually really good for
me, because I don't have a really good job,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.440
and I couldn't really afford the mortgage
payments."

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.130
"Oh, do we have a deal for you! 
Check this out: we'll give you, like,

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:54.070
no mortgage payments for, like, two years!
And you won't have to pay any loans at all!

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.230
You just - it's free! It's like a free house!"

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:02.890
"Oh my God, two years? Well, in two years I'll probably be, like, the CEO of my company!

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.040
And my house will obviously be worth, like,
fifty percent more, so

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.080
what am I really concerned about here?"

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.040
"Uh, nothing! You have nothing to worry about
at all. Here's your loan!"

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.945
"Thank you!"

00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:16.350
"Boy, am I glad I don't actually
 have to secure that loan!

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.380
I'll just send it off to the bank, and they'll have some

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.870
solution for what to do with this 
crappy loan that I just made."

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.320
Meanwhile, at the bank:

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.500
"We're giving out a lot of loans ... but we're
not seein' a lot of income from those loans,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.990
but we need to give out more loans, so what
do we do? How do we get more capitol,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.280
so we can give more bad loans?"

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.770
"Well, I think I have an idea for us. 
How about we do this:

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:41.170
we take the loans that we've got,
and then we sell them.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.850
We sell them as assets.
We say,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.409
'These loans are going to be paid back, and
if they're not, then the houses that they're on

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:51.049
will be worth way more in the end, anyway!

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:54.390
And, so, the loans will have
 a great deal of worth.'

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.330
We'll sell them off, and people will buy them
up like hot cakes, and they'll be so excited!

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.045
And here's an idea: 
we'll give them different names, 

00:02:01.145 --> 00:02:03.160
so the bad mortages we'll name 'Triple-A',

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.940
and we'll say they're the best of the bad
mortgages.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.980
And then, the really bad mortgages 
we'll name 'Triple-B',

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.049
which sounds way better than 
'Really, Really Bad Mortgages'!

00:02:12.149 --> 00:02:15.650
And then the ones that stink like elephant
diarrhea on a hot day -

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.320
we'll just not name those ones at all, and
give people really great interest rates on

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.740
those securities."

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.960
"Oh, my God, that kinda sounds like a good idea!

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.270
So people will just buy 'em, 
and then we'll get more money,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.615
so we can loan more money out!

00:02:26.715 --> 00:02:28.160
...But, wait a second: if the bad mortgages

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.570
all turn out to be bad,
aren't we just screwing ourselves? I mean,

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.610
yeah, we end up making more money 
in the short term,

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:36.520
because now we can make a loan, and then make money on that loan by selling the loan,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.959
but, in the long term, aren't we gonna
be screwed?"

00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:42.840
"Oh, you have the wrong vision! You see, you're
looking into the future.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.290
What you need to do is think about next quarter,

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.440
because that's what our investors are thinking about!

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.039
You need to think about 
how much money we will be making

00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:52.810
in the immediate future!
Thinking two or three years down the line

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.910
is not something we do here in the financial
sector.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.270
And, besides, housing prices will always go
up, and if not, the government will be there

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.035
to catch us."

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:01.599
"Why would the government be there to catch us?"

00:03:01.699 --> 00:03:03.850
"Because, my friend, if we go down, 

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.825
the country goes down. 
Don't worry; they'll be there."

00:03:06.925 --> 00:03:08.500
Two years later...

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.850
"Aawww, I can't pay this mortgage payment...
And my house is worth way less!?

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.750
Hold on a second, let me get my calculator!

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:18.950
Dwah! I knew it!

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.690
My mortgage is worth way more than my house
is!

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.280
I could default on my mortgage, and then get
another mortgage,

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.630
and get a way better house for the same price!

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.980
I'm totally gonna stop 
paying these mortgage payments!"

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.000
Back at the bank:

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:30.985
"Hello...?"

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:32.969
"Hello, my name is Jeremy Hogart, and I bought

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:36.250
a bunch of your mortgage-backed securities,
and I have recently discovered that they are

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.190
worth absolutely nothing. 
What the freak is going ooon?"

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.820
"Very much apologies
for the inconvenience,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.920
yes, lots of people bought them.
In fact, people bought them recently

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.380
in the economies across the world,
and - and it's possible that we may have triggered

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.710
somewhat of a tiny,

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.540
massive global recession.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.975
Very sorry, working on it right now...
We'll get back to you. 

00:03:53.075 --> 00:03:55.710
Please, do not call again. Bye."

